      model_answer: 'That is a fair question. The old catalogue card gives Bell’s name. When we checked the original register, we could see that Bell was paid for plans and that someone else was paid for copying. The entry does not say who drew this particular sheet, and we can see marks that may have been made by more than one person. So there is a good reason to keep Bell’s name, although the wording leaves room for the other contributor. In this case, the label tells you which connection we can trace and which part of the story we are still trying to establish. We’ll revisit it if we find stronger evidence.',
